bodyText:
Michigan lets you retake possession and dispose of the contents after a sole tenant dies, without liability for unlawful interference, only if all of these are met:
- you informed the tenant in writing of the option to name a contact person for that event;
- current rent is unpaid;
- you believe in good faith that the tenant has been dead at least 18 days and no other tenant survives;
- and at least 10 days before reentry you attempted to reach the named contact and ask that a probate estate be opened within 28 days after death, posted on the door a notice of your intent to reenter and dispose of the contents after 10 days, and notified the county public administrator (or, if none, the state public administrator), giving that administrator access on request.

You may not proceed if a probate estate has been opened in the county or you have been notified in writing of one opened elsewhere.

Michigan requires these landlord-tenant notices in writing:
- a 7-day notice to quit for nonpayment, the 24-hour notice to quit after a controlled-substance termination, and the 7-day notice to quit after physical injury or a threat of it;
- every demand for possession or payment (stating the premises, the reasons, the time to cure, any amount due, dated and signed);
- your security deposit notice within 14 days after move-in;
- the tenant's forwarding address within 4 days after moving out;
- your itemized notice of damages, mailed within 30 days after the tenancy ends, and the tenant's response by mail within 7 days;
- your notice of transfer of the deposit to a new owner;
- at least 30 days' written notice of any lease adjustment the law allows during the term;
- your notice curing a void lease term, and the tenant's notice objecting to one;
- the tenant's 60-day notice ending the lease for senior housing or infirmity;
- the tenant's notice seeking a domestic violence release, by certified mail;
- and the written option you give the tenant to name a contact in case of death.

The ordinary 1-month notice ending a periodic tenancy is not expressly required to be written by statute, but an eviction complaint must attach copies of any notice to quit and any demand for possession showing when and how they were served, so give every notice in writing.

A demand for possession may be served by personal delivery, delivery to a household member or employee of suitable age at the premises, first-class mail, or electronic service only with the tenant's specific written consent.
